  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 335 AM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nA stalled front will act as a warm front today across the state\nas a surface low lifts across central Canada. On the warm side\nof this front will be highs in the 60s, with some breezy\nsoutherly winds. Along and north of this front will be cooler\nyet still above normal highs in the 50s, with not as breezy of\nwinds. Some southwestern areas could see increased fire weather\nconcerns this afternoon, although at this time critical fire \nweather conditions are not anticipated. Abundant dry air will\nkeep conditions dry, with some mid to high clouds at times. A\nweak and dry cold front then moves through tonight, which could\nbring some increased cloud cover. This increased cloud cover\nwill also bring more mild lows in the mid 30s to lower 40s.\nDepending on location, these would approach record warm minimum\nrecords for this time of year. A breezy and dry post frontal day\nis then expected for Monday. This weak cold front will only cool\ntemperatures slightly and bring highs still in the 50s.\nAfternoon humidity values should remain above 30 percent for\nmost areas, although some southwestern areas may see some\nincreased fire weather concerns. Confidence in critical fire\nweather conditions remains low at this time. Surface high then\nlessens winds Monday night and brings cooler lowers in the 20s\nand 30s. A passing wave to our north will push another weak cold\nfront south on Tuesday. This combined with a broad troughing\npattern may bring a few isolated rain showers. More breezy\nwesterly winds are possible, mainly in the southwest. Slightly\ncooler temperatures will be found as a result of this additional\ncold front with highs in the mid 40s to mid 50s. Another surface\nhigh then moves in for Tuesday night into Wednesday morning\nlingering lows in the 20s and 30s. There could be some trapped\nlow level moisture associated with this surface high and behind\nTuesday's front. Perhaps this brings some increased low clouds\nor even patchy fog, however, confidence is not high enough to\ninclude in the forecast at this time.\n\nWednesday through next weekend will likely be under the\ninfluence of an amplified ridge moving into the central US. \nSome larger temperature spreads remain for later in the work \nweek as this ridge forms. However once the ridge builds over the\ncentral US there is increased confidence in above normal \ntemperatures for the upcoming weekend. Dry conditions will also \nfollow this ridge through the upcoming weekend. Although some \nlower RH may be found with these warmer temperatures, a lack of \nwind should limit fire weather concerns through the end of the \nweek.",
